            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Cuts a sequence into windows of a given length - overlapping or not - and gives them all at once, one window per row. Every 30-frame window of a movement, ready to be averaged, measured or fed to a model in one step instead of one window at a time.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.unfold <window> <step>\nDefaults: 4, 1.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe sequence. Every input sends.\n\nwindow (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many values in each window.\n\nstep (int box, also an inlet):\nHow far each window starts after the last: 1 for every possible window; the window length for windows side by side with no overlap.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nThe dim the windows run along; -1, the last, by default. For frames x joints, use 0:\nthe result is windows x joints x window.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nwindows:\nOne more dim than the input: the windows, then the values in each. A copy, so changing it does not change the input.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n0 1 2 3 4 5 in windows of 3: every window, each starting one later (top); with step 3, side by side (bottom).\n\nRELATED:\nnp.sliding_window_view is the numpy version. t.rolling_buffer keeps the latest window of a stream. t.mean with a dim then averages each window. t.stft analyses each window's frequencies.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1150,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Cuts a sequence into windows of a given length - overlapping or not - and gives them all at once, one window per row. Every 30-frame window of a movement, ready to be averaged, measured or fed to a model in one step instead of one window at a time.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.unfold <window> <step>\nDefaults: 4, 1.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe sequence. Every input sends.\n\nwindow (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many values in each window.\n\nstep (int box, also an inlet):\nHow far each window starts after the last: 1 for every possible window; the window length for windows side by side with no overlap.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nThe dim the windows run along; -1, the last, by default. For frames x joints, use 0:\nthe result is windows x joints x window.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nwindows:\nOne more dim than the input: the windows, then the values in each. A copy, so changing it does not change the input.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n0 1 2 3 4 5 in windows of 3: every window, each starting one later (top); with step 3, side by side (bottom).\n\nRELATED:\nnp.sliding_window_view is the numpy version. t.rolling_buffer keeps the latest window of a stream. t.mean with a dim then averages each window. t.stft analyses each window's frequencies."
        },
